<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nefertiti</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1370–c. 1330 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century-bce">14th century BCE</a> · Also known as Neferneferuaten · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eighteenth-dynasty-of-egypt">Eighteenth Dynasty of Egypt</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nefertiti was a queen of the 18th Dynasty of Ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, the great royal wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">Pharaoh</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhenaten">Akhenaten</a>. With her husband she overhauled state religion, promoting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atenism">Atenism</a>, a possibly monotheistic religion centred on the sun disc. Almost nothing is known of her life before her marriage; the couple had at least six daughters, among them Meritaten and Ankhesenpaaten, later the wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tutankhamun">Tutankhamun</a>. Some scholars believe that after Akhenaten's death she ruled briefly as the female pharaoh Neferneferuaten, an identification still debated. Her bust, attributed to the sculptor Thutmose and excavated from his studio in the early 20th century, is now in the Neues Museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nefertiti.jpg" alt="Nefertiti" width="330" height="483" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Philip Pikart · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nofretete_Neues_Museum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Her name was a sentence meaning the beautiful one has come. Tombs at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhetaten">Amarna</a> mention a sister named Mutbenret, and a woman called Tey bore the title Nurse of the Great Royal Wife. Because Tey's husband Ay was God's Father, some Egyptologists have proposed that he was Nefertiti's father, though neither is named as her parent in the sources and the proposal rests on conjecture. Other theories, that she was Amenhotep IV's sister or the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitanni">Mitanni</a> princess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tadukhipa">Tadukhipa</a>, are contradicted by her titles or lack any evidence of a foreign background.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Nefertiti first appears at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a>, in the tomb of the vizier Ramose, standing behind Amenhotep IV. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnak">Karnak</a> a structure called the Mansion of the Benben was dedicated to her. Among scenes on talatat blocks from the first five years of his reign she appears almost twice as often as the king, shown smiting enemies, and captive enemies decorate her throne. In his fourth year Amenhotep IV decided to move the capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhetaten">Akhetaten</a>, modern Amarna, and in his fifth he took the name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhenaten">Akhenaten</a> while she became Neferneferuaten-Nefertiti.</p><p>The last dated inscription naming both comes from the limestone quarry at Deir Abu Hinnis, in year 16 of the king's reign, and was found in 2012. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhenaten">Akhenaten</a> died in his 17th year.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Many Egyptologists, among them Aidan Dodson and James Allen, now think the female pharaoh Neferneferuaten who ruled between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhenaten">Akhenaten</a> and Tutankhaten was Nefertiti, since she had borne that name from the early years of the reign. Her death and burial are uncertain. The Younger Lady mummy in KV35, proposed as hers in 2003 by Joann Fletcher, was shown by scans and DNA to be Tutankhaten's mother, and after Nicholas Reeves proposed in 2015 that voids behind the walls of Tutankhaten's tomb hid her burial chamber, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radar">radar</a> scans showed there were none.</p><p>A Hittite letter records a widowed Egyptian queen asking for one of the king's sons as husband. She may have been Nefertiti, Meritaten or Ankhesenamun, and nothing is settled. Her bust, made by the sculptor Thutmose, was found in 1912.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nefertiti” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
